WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.325
It is worth noting that cats
have really small lungs,

00:00:07.325 --> 00:00:09.818
and if they fail to connect with that prey

00:00:09.818 --> 00:00:12.480
and to take it down quickly, they give up.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.112
So if you've got a long head start,

00:00:15.112 --> 00:00:15.999
you're going to outrun it.

00:00:15.999 --> 00:00:17.341
My name's Mark Elbroch.

00:00:17.341 --> 00:00:19.590
I've been studying mountain
lions for 20 years,

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:21.060
and I've been lucky enough to also work

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:22.668
with African lions and leopards.

00:00:22.668 --> 00:00:25.303
Today, we're going to be
looking at big-cat attacks

00:00:25.303 --> 00:00:27.828
in movies and judging how real they are.

00:00:36.291 --> 00:00:40.622
Where they stand up to begin
a wrestling is actually real.

00:00:40.622 --> 00:00:43.644
African lions will do that
when they engage in a fight,

00:00:43.644 --> 00:00:46.871
especially over territory
or rights to a pride.

00:00:46.871 --> 00:00:50.001
Young lions actually challenge old lions.

00:00:50.001 --> 00:00:53.550
That's totally real. And that old lions

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:57.300
almost always have a set
of scars on their face

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:00.381
from these territorial disputes over time.

00:01:00.381 --> 00:01:01.728
That's totally normal.

00:01:01.728 --> 00:01:04.140
To see an old lion without any scars

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.373
means that lion has
been bred in captivity.

00:01:08.764 --> 00:01:10.740
The swiping of the paws,

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:13.873
all of that sort of rapid violence

00:01:13.873 --> 00:01:16.059
is very real.

00:01:16.059 --> 00:01:19.578
I would just point out,
too, that the skin of cats,

00:01:19.578 --> 00:01:21.487
when you see them, they often look loose.

00:01:21.487 --> 00:01:25.142
They're swiping with these weapons,

00:01:25.142 --> 00:01:27.930
these claws that can
dig in an inch or more,

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:30.570
and that loose skin is protecting them,

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:32.897
the big mane is protecting them,

00:01:32.897 --> 00:01:35.270
so that all is defenses

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:38.153
to allow them to have
these violent exchanges.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:45.247
There was a moment there where
you saw one go onto his back.

00:01:45.247 --> 00:01:46.374
That's really normal.

00:01:46.374 --> 00:01:50.661
A cat in a defensive posture
will start to go onto its back

00:01:50.661 --> 00:01:52.650
to have four weapons pointing up.

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:54.030
They are devastating because now,

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:57.270
if a competitor comes on top of them,

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:00.930
they just shred their underbelly,
with all the vital organs.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:04.290
It's amazing to have that
much realism in a cartoon,

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.015
so I'm going to give this thing an eight.

00:02:11.604 --> 00:02:13.791
This is a pet animal, a pet tiger.

00:02:13.791 --> 00:02:18.540
There are more tigers in
captivity in the US alone

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:21.567
than there are wild tigers
in the entire world,

00:02:21.567 --> 00:02:24.000
and that's just a crazy thing to ponder.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.491
The ears were forward.

00:02:25.491 --> 00:02:27.420
You always watch the ears of cats.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:29.415
And so this was not a threatened animal,

00:02:29.415 --> 00:02:32.368
it wasn't an agitated
animal, it was just chilling.

00:02:32.368 --> 00:02:34.885
Stu: This does not seem fair.

00:02:34.885 --> 00:02:37.218
Phil: It's rock, paper, scissors.
There's nothing more fair.

00:02:37.218 --> 00:02:39.591
Interesting idea to try to drug the tiger.

00:02:39.591 --> 00:02:43.968
In fact, I know people who have
tried this on wild animals.

00:02:43.968 --> 00:02:46.821
They use syringes and
load them up with drugs.

00:02:46.821 --> 00:02:49.790
I've seen that work in the field.

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:52.588
So that's real. I mean, real-ish,

00:02:52.588 --> 00:02:53.763
depending what they're giving it.

00:02:56.657 --> 00:02:57.690
Oh, my God!

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:00.600
When you saw the tiger
chewing on the seat,

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.464
we call those displacement behaviors.

00:03:02.464 --> 00:03:05.880
A lot of captive animals
exhibit sort of stress

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:06.904
in different ways,

00:03:06.904 --> 00:03:09.564
and so they'll bite things,
they'll claw things.

00:03:09.564 --> 00:03:13.022
You know, I'm going to
give this movie a five.

00:03:14.096 --> 00:03:16.743
Oh, dear Santo.

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:19.483
Note the pattern here,

00:03:19.483 --> 00:03:23.241
that there's sort of black
squares with dots in them.

00:03:23.241 --> 00:03:27.140
And that's really important
to identifying a jaguar

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:29.557
and differentiating it from a leopard,

00:03:29.557 --> 00:03:31.319
which has no black dots in the middle.

00:03:37.282 --> 00:03:40.365
I've seen this movie, so
I know the context of it,

00:03:40.365 --> 00:03:42.329
and so I know that the jaguar is a pet.

00:03:42.329 --> 00:03:45.960
The owner has trained this cat

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:48.120
to engage in wrestling matches.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:50.997
Would a jaguar wander into a bar

00:03:50.997 --> 00:03:53.550
to get a raw steak or to engage people?

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:54.648
Absolutely not.

00:03:54.648 --> 00:03:58.770
In fact, jaguars, among all the big cats,

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:01.603
have the least records

00:04:01.603 --> 00:04:04.069
of attacking people in the wild.

00:04:04.069 --> 00:04:05.986
It's almost unheard of.

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:12.061
If that were a real fight

00:04:12.061 --> 00:04:14.880
and the jaguar were truly to bite down,

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:17.740
it could quite literally
bite through his arm.

00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:20.400
They have tremendous biting power

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:22.993
as compared to other cats of similar size.

00:04:22.993 --> 00:04:25.293
[jaguar purring]

00:04:25.293 --> 00:04:26.908
I'm going to pet you now,

00:04:26.908 --> 00:04:29.727
and you're not going
to eat me, understood?

00:04:29.727 --> 00:04:31.061
You know, Hollywood loves its noises,

00:04:31.061 --> 00:04:34.598
and in this case, it
has the jaguar purring.

00:04:34.598 --> 00:04:37.980
Jaguars can roar, but they cannot purr,

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:40.262
so that, of course, is
completely unrealistic.

00:04:40.262 --> 00:04:43.480
I'm going to give this a four.

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:46.992
It's got tremendous comedy
and poor natural history.

00:04:46.992 --> 00:04:49.928
Hey, buddy. Huh, huh?

00:04:49.928 --> 00:04:51.601
You a little scary kitty, huh?

00:04:51.601 --> 00:04:52.914
That's a real mountain lion kitten.

00:04:52.914 --> 00:04:55.380
All cats are born with blue eyes.

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:58.433
The pigments haven't started
to develop in their eyes yet.

00:04:58.433 --> 00:05:01.673
You can see this kitten
has been handled plenty.

00:05:01.673 --> 00:05:04.740
It's not agitated. Its ears are forward.

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:06.599
To me, I would call that a smiling kitty.

00:05:09.542 --> 00:05:10.920
[mountain lion roars]

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:13.868
Mountain lions can't roar,
so they were often considered

00:05:13.868 --> 00:05:15.479
the largest of the small cats.

00:05:15.479 --> 00:05:18.106
An agitated mountain
lion would be hissing.

00:05:18.106 --> 00:05:21.226
They go [hisses], and then
they, often, they'll snort.

00:05:21.226 --> 00:05:23.760
Like, as they pull the
air back in to hiss again,

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:25.982
they'll go [snorts] and go [snorts].

00:05:25.982 --> 00:05:28.004
Oh, my God.

00:05:28.004 --> 00:05:29.200
[mountain lion growls]

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:30.358
Help! Help!

00:05:30.358 --> 00:05:32.160
This is one of the first clips

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:36.262
in which we've seen a cat drop its head

00:05:36.262 --> 00:05:38.640
and lower the front half of its body,

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:42.270
and that's exactly what a wildcat does

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:44.174
when it is preparing to pounce.

00:05:44.174 --> 00:05:46.500
What would a mountain lion really do

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:48.180
if it was defending its kittens?

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:50.790
It's all about bluster.

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:52.939
They'll false-charge you. They make noise.

00:05:52.939 --> 00:05:56.132
It would be an incredibly rare thing

00:05:56.132 --> 00:05:58.199
that they would actually make contact,

00:05:58.199 --> 00:06:01.320
because in a fight they are vulnerable,

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:03.300
and the best thing for the safety

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:05.492
of their own kittens is to stay alive.

00:06:07.474 --> 00:06:09.365
Oh! Oh, my God

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:13.261
I mean, there's so much to talk about.

00:06:13.261 --> 00:06:14.951
The cat's enormous to Kevin,

00:06:14.951 --> 00:06:18.480
and I know Kevin Hart is not a huge man.

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:21.120
So, a typical female, especially
in Southern California,

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:22.440
is going to be, like, 80 pounds.

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:25.640
That cat is huge compared
to what it should be.

00:06:27.229 --> 00:06:29.827
Oh. Oh, my God.

00:06:29.827 --> 00:06:31.111
How she even dropped to the ground

00:06:31.111 --> 00:06:35.624
and let go of him is
totally not mountain lion.

00:06:35.624 --> 00:06:38.657
When a cat attaches to
something it's attacking,

00:06:38.657 --> 00:06:42.507
they do not let go.
Like, they are attached,

00:06:42.507 --> 00:06:44.246
and they'll go down with their prey.

00:06:44.246 --> 00:06:47.450
They've actually been
seen rolling off mountains

00:06:47.450 --> 00:06:49.383
attached to a bighorn sheep

00:06:49.383 --> 00:06:52.016
or some other prey and rolling together.

00:06:55.081 --> 00:06:56.343
To the jugular!

00:06:57.235 --> 00:06:58.978
Is that an EpiPen?

00:06:58.978 --> 00:07:01.059
He's not jabbing it in the jugular.

00:07:01.059 --> 00:07:05.212
He's jabbing it in the
shoulder, which would be

00:07:05.212 --> 00:07:07.950
the least effective form of defense.

00:07:07.950 --> 00:07:09.930
The neck would've been good, or the eye.

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:12.816
People often recommend, if
a cat is actually attacking,

00:07:12.816 --> 00:07:13.855
you go for the eye.

00:07:13.855 --> 00:07:17.246
I'm going to give this one a five.

00:07:21.370 --> 00:07:24.120
African lions, the largest ones,

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:27.492
are almost as large as the largest tigers.

00:07:27.492 --> 00:07:29.967
There are records of up to 600 pounds,

00:07:29.967 --> 00:07:32.616
but they exhibit extreme dimorphism,

00:07:32.616 --> 00:07:35.275
and that's a fancy word
for saying the males

00:07:35.275 --> 00:07:37.127
are a lot bigger than the females.

00:07:37.127 --> 00:07:40.760
When I've been in the field
and confronted a lion,

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:44.239
when they stand up, their
eyes are essentially

00:07:44.239 --> 00:07:48.538
even with your chest, which
is incredibly intimidating.

00:07:52.002 --> 00:07:54.035
This is hard to watch.

00:07:54.035 --> 00:07:55.941
This reminds me of,

00:07:55.941 --> 00:07:58.290
is it "The Revenant" that
has Leonardo DiCaprio?

00:07:58.290 --> 00:08:01.860
That bear scene was just
brutal in its ferocity,

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:02.693
and this is,

00:08:02.693 --> 00:08:05.426
I mean, this is the most
realistic attack we've seen.

00:08:05.426 --> 00:08:07.590
I think he's on his back.

00:08:07.590 --> 00:08:10.198
They often go for the spine, big cats,

00:08:10.198 --> 00:08:11.801
when they're attacking any kind of prey.

00:08:11.801 --> 00:08:14.192
And you notice this lion is biting a lot.

00:08:14.192 --> 00:08:15.938
We haven't seen that in these other clips.

00:08:15.938 --> 00:08:18.153
Lots of clawing, which, as I talked about,

00:08:18.153 --> 00:08:20.280
is more about wounding.

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:22.489
This is a lion going for a kill.

00:08:22.489 --> 00:08:24.740
This clip is as real as it gets.

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:26.980
I'm going to give it a solid eight,

00:08:26.980 --> 00:08:30.720
and it loses two points in just the impact

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:33.116
on the person who was
at the receiving end.

00:08:33.116 --> 00:08:35.524
You know, the devastation of that attack

00:08:35.524 --> 00:08:37.421
would have been lethal.

00:08:37.421 --> 00:08:40.092
[mountain lion growls and yowls]

00:08:40.092 --> 00:08:41.125
What the hell?

00:08:41.125 --> 00:08:44.334
Reese: But if you're scared,
that beautiful death machine

00:08:44.334 --> 00:08:46.907
will do what God made it to do.

00:08:46.907 --> 00:08:51.117
This part of that clip is actually real.

00:08:51.117 --> 00:08:54.240
You know, that if you keep
showing fear to a mountain lion

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:57.359
or to another big cat,
you're setting yourself up

00:08:57.359 --> 00:08:59.509
for a potentially dangerous situation.

00:08:59.509 --> 00:09:02.924
You never want to give
a cat the opportunity

00:09:02.924 --> 00:09:05.070
to what I call gain momentum.

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:08.499
They'll become bolder,
and they'll continue to do

00:09:08.499 --> 00:09:10.516
whatever they're doing to make you afraid.

00:09:10.516 --> 00:09:13.110
Most folks will recommend
you take two fast steps

00:09:13.110 --> 00:09:16.095
towards it. Like, you step
towards it, and I'll clap.

00:09:16.095 --> 00:09:20.580
I'll stomp my feet, I'll be
loud, I'll be aggressive,

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:22.900
and they'll usually just be like, "Whoa,"

00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:24.360
and then they'll leave.

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:25.920
God, he's just following me wherever I go.

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:27.198
Reese: Well, he's just looking at you.

00:09:27.198 --> 00:09:29.880
That's actually a really
cool thing to talk about,

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:33.461
because cats lock on their prey.

00:09:33.461 --> 00:09:37.058
To get them to break that
focal lock is really important

00:09:37.058 --> 00:09:40.448
if you were to ever be in
an encounter with a big cat,

00:09:40.448 --> 00:09:43.485
which you can do by yelling, screaming,

00:09:43.485 --> 00:09:44.993
doing something surprising.

00:09:44.993 --> 00:09:46.777
Hey, here we go. I'm getting in.

00:09:46.777 --> 00:09:49.392
[mountain lion growls and hisses]

00:09:49.392 --> 00:09:52.633
The sound at the end,
that [growls and hisses],

00:09:52.633 --> 00:09:55.572
it's so funny because it's
the ringtone on my phone,

00:09:55.572 --> 00:09:58.626
because that is one of the most overused

00:09:58.626 --> 00:10:01.176
popular sounds of mountain lions.

00:10:01.176 --> 00:10:04.678
I love the fact they talked
about fear. We'll give it a six.

00:10:05.570 --> 00:10:10.083
[tiger roars]

00:10:10.083 --> 00:10:14.488
The greatest problem with
Hollywood cat attacks

00:10:14.488 --> 00:10:15.643
are the noises.

00:10:15.643 --> 00:10:19.080
First off, cats are completely
silent when they're hunting.

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:21.113
You don't let your prey
know you're coming.

00:10:23.757 --> 00:10:27.221
When that tiger emerges and hits the wolf,

00:10:27.221 --> 00:10:29.127
that's actually a really important thing

00:10:29.127 --> 00:10:32.889
to note about cat hunting, is
that they use their bodies,

00:10:32.889 --> 00:10:35.297
not just their claws and their teeth.

00:10:35.297 --> 00:10:39.000
Sometimes, if they can get
enough power behind themselves,

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:41.842
just their weight alone can
break the neck of an animal.

00:10:44.746 --> 00:10:46.369
You should never run.

00:10:46.369 --> 00:10:49.860
That's like triggering the prey response

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:51.241
in a large carnivore.

00:10:51.241 --> 00:10:54.990
You want to confront a large
cat that is confronting you.

00:10:54.990 --> 00:10:57.226
If it's threatening you.
You want to scare it.

00:10:57.226 --> 00:11:00.666
But let's just assume that you're running.

00:11:00.666 --> 00:11:02.484
You know, you've made that decision.

00:11:02.484 --> 00:11:05.726
It is worth noting that
cats have really small lungs

00:11:05.726 --> 00:11:07.469
and that they have these short bursts.

00:11:07.469 --> 00:11:09.551
That's why they're
explosive when they hunt.

00:11:09.551 --> 00:11:12.081
And if they fail to connect with that prey

00:11:12.081 --> 00:11:14.760
and to take it down quickly, they give up.

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:17.292
So if you've got a long head start

00:11:17.292 --> 00:11:20.400
and you could get that
cat to run for, you know,

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:24.030
half mile or so, yeah,
you're going to outrun it,

00:11:24.030 --> 00:11:28.024
because humans are actually
built for marathon runs.

00:11:32.210 --> 00:11:34.800
There's no way he could stand up

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:36.270
to being clawed several times.

00:11:36.270 --> 00:11:40.530
So, cat claws are protractible,
and that means that

00:11:40.530 --> 00:11:42.870
when the cat is relaxed,
they're actually sheathed.

00:11:42.870 --> 00:11:46.290
You don't see the claws. And
that keeps them razor sharp

00:11:46.290 --> 00:11:50.730
so that when they capture
prey or swipe prey,

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:54.690
they immediately cut
in and cut lacerations,

00:11:54.690 --> 00:11:59.206
like sharp knives. Just
one swipe of a tiger's claw

00:11:59.206 --> 00:12:02.372
would completely debilitate this guy.

00:12:02.372 --> 00:12:06.466
I'm going to give this one a seven.

00:12:06.466 --> 00:12:09.491
Come on! Come on!

00:12:09.491 --> 00:12:10.902
Come on!

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:17.776
Will a tiger defend food

00:12:17.776 --> 00:12:19.834
from a potential scavenger?

00:12:19.834 --> 00:12:22.080
Absolutely, and lions too,

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:25.428
and mountain lions too, and leopards too.

00:12:25.428 --> 00:12:26.276
They'll all do that.

00:12:31.243 --> 00:12:33.930
It has the tiger's ears out to the side,

00:12:33.930 --> 00:12:36.778
and that is an uncomfortable animal.

00:12:36.778 --> 00:12:38.315
That is an agitated animal.

00:12:38.315 --> 00:12:40.860
It is telling the world in every way

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:44.685
that it is very unhappy
with its current situation.

00:12:50.996 --> 00:12:53.357
Pi takes to water for safety, right?

00:12:53.357 --> 00:12:57.648
He leaps off the boat, and if
the tiger were locked on him,

00:12:57.648 --> 00:12:59.534
like, truly hunting him,

00:12:59.534 --> 00:13:01.082
that probably wouldn't have saved him

00:13:01.082 --> 00:13:03.750
because tigers swim and swim quite well.

00:13:03.750 --> 00:13:06.424
But in this case, as I mentioned,

00:13:06.424 --> 00:13:08.992
the tiger didn't seem to be locked on him.

00:13:08.992 --> 00:13:12.090
I'm going to give this clip an eight

00:13:12.090 --> 00:13:15.031
because the CGI is amazing.

00:13:21.300 --> 00:13:22.133
That could happen.

00:13:22.133 --> 00:13:24.870
I've seen mountain lions kill bear cubs

00:13:24.870 --> 00:13:26.220
on a couple of occasions.

00:13:26.220 --> 00:13:29.521
In terms of how it would
hunt in open terrain,

00:13:29.521 --> 00:13:32.400
the most similar sort
of scenario I've seen

00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:35.070
is in the far south out in
the Patagonia grasslands,

00:13:35.070 --> 00:13:38.838
and a mountain lion would
be down low, using every bit

00:13:38.838 --> 00:13:42.744
of grass or rock to get
as close as possible.

00:13:42.744 --> 00:13:44.413
They wouldn't betray their presence.

00:13:44.413 --> 00:13:47.640
Now, if they thought that
prey was totally vulnerable,

00:13:47.640 --> 00:13:51.450
which a young bear might be, then sure,

00:13:51.450 --> 00:13:54.677
you'll see cats nonchalantly approach prey

00:13:54.677 --> 00:13:56.310
that cannot get away.

00:14:00.594 --> 00:14:03.180
We can appreciate this is
an actual mountain lion

00:14:03.180 --> 00:14:06.759
but that this is a cat that
is clearly uncomfortable

00:14:06.759 --> 00:14:07.776
and is not his hunting.

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:16.862
Would a mountain lion, confronted with

00:14:16.862 --> 00:14:20.499
an adult brown bear, turn and flee?

00:14:20.499 --> 00:14:21.996
Absolutely.

00:14:21.996 --> 00:14:25.350
You know, mountain lions
will always choose the exit

00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:28.230
rather than engage
unless they're cornered,

00:14:28.230 --> 00:14:29.940
and that gets me a little bit

00:14:29.940 --> 00:14:31.350
to talk about the habitat here.

00:14:31.350 --> 00:14:35.550
It's totally open. It's hardly any trees.

00:14:35.550 --> 00:14:36.960
It's a lot of rock.

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:40.601
This is actually not great cat habitat,

00:14:40.601 --> 00:14:44.892
at least in the north
where you have brown bears,

00:14:44.892 --> 00:14:48.030
because this is the domain of wolves,

00:14:48.030 --> 00:14:51.821
and wolves always have the
upper hand in the open.

00:14:51.821 --> 00:14:53.664
And so cats, when you look at

00:14:53.664 --> 00:14:55.632
their distribution across North America,

00:14:55.632 --> 00:14:59.250
they're almost never found
in these wide-open areas.

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:02.700
So, we've got real
mountain lions, real bears.

00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:05.460
I'm still going to give it a seven

00:15:05.460 --> 00:15:08.850
because the open terrain
doesn't really match.

00:15:08.850 --> 00:15:11.670
My favorite big-cat-attack scene

00:15:11.670 --> 00:15:13.950
from today would be from "Me Time."

00:15:13.950 --> 00:15:15.990
Part of my enjoyment of that clip

00:15:15.990 --> 00:15:19.996
was how poorly it was done in terms of CGI

00:15:19.996 --> 00:15:23.383
and the interaction between
Kevin Hart's character

00:15:23.383 --> 00:15:24.839
and the actual mountain lion.

00:15:24.839 --> 00:15:26.431
Thanks so much for watching,

00:15:26.431 --> 00:15:29.747
and if you're felining it,

00:15:29.747 --> 00:15:31.314
please click on the video above.

